                24
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "aHczW",
                  "name": "t1",
                  "fill": "#0E2747",
                  "content": "Eigenvalues of Q",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 20,
                  "fontWeight": "800"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "w7UYKJ",
                  "name": "t2",
                  "fill": "#0E2747",
                  "content": "What do eigenvalues of Q measure?",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 15,
                  "fontWeight": "700"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "OmHJU",
                  "name": "t2b",
                  "fill": "#5B6680",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Each eigenvalue tracks how fast a specific card-position bias fades. Eigenvalue 1 = uniform (never decays). Others = non-random biases that vanish after repeated steps.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.5,
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 12,
                  "fontWeight": "500"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "vBmzY",
                  "name": "evCalcTitle",
                  "fill": "#0E2747",
                  "content": "How to calculate eigenvalues",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 15,
                  "fontWeight": "700"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "SGTiN",
                  "name": "evCalcBody",
                  "fill": "#5B6680",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Q is built from the generator structure: each entry Q[i,j] is the probability a card at position i moves to position j when one random generator is applied. Its eigenvalues are the special values λ where Q·v = λ·v, the directions that Q only scales, never rotates. For our 5-card deck with 4 neighbor-swap generators, these work out to:",
                  "lineHeight": 1.5,
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 12,
                  "fontWeight": "500"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "NCewc",
                  "name": "evList",
                  "fill": "#E8573F",
                  "content": "λ₀ = 1    λ₁ = 0.905    λ₂ = 0.655    λ₃ = 0.345    λ₄ = 0.095",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 13,
                  "fontWeight": "700"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "oAqy6",
                  "name": "rwTitle",
                  "fill": "#0E2747",
                  "content": "Why L steps of matrix multiplication?",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 13,
                  "fontWeight": "700"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "zAfhn",
                  "name": "rwBody",
                  "fill": "#5B6680",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Each step = pick a random generator, apply it. Q[i,j] = probability a card at position i moves to j in one step. So Q^L[i,j] = probability after L steps. As L grows, every row converges to (1/5, ..., 1/5) — the walk forgets where it started.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.5,
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 11,
                  "fontWeight": "500"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "yR71b",
                  "name": "t3",
                  "fill": "#0E2747",
                  "content": "The spectral gap",
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 15,
                  "fontWeight": "700"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "jNyEY",
                  "name": "t3b",
                  "fill": "#5B6680",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Gap between eigenvalue 1 and the next (0.905). This bottleneck — the slowest-decaying pattern — controls how many steps are needed.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.5,
                  "fontFamily": "Inter",
                  "fontSize": 12,
                  "fontWeight": "500"
                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "e4OIxN",
                  "name": "eigenChart",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "height": 120,
                  "layout": "none",
